c -l events.csv && head -15 events.csv"], ["Bash", "cat README.md && cat crontab.txt"]], "final_message": "Very likely cause: the `ts` column in `events.csv` mixes three timestamp formats, and a charting tool will place them at different times. There's no chart code in this repo, so I only looked at the data. The file has just 3 rows:\n\n```\nid,ts\n1,2026-03-01T10:00:00Z            \u2190 UTC (explicit \"Z\")\n2,2026-03-01T10:05:00             \u2190 no timezone (naive)\n3,2026-03-01T04:10:00-06:00       \u2190 UTC-6 offset\n```\n\n- **Row 3 is really 10:10 UTC.** 04:10 at -06:00 is 10:10Z. Read as local or naive time, it lands about 6 hours before rows 1 and 2, which fits the jumps of several hours you're seeing.\n- **Row 2 has no zone, so its meaning depends on the reader.** pandas, JS `Date`, Excel and SQL loaders each treat naive times differently. Some assume UTC and some assume the machine's local zone. Others fail to parse it, or drop the offset from the other rows. So the same file can chart differently depending on where it's loaded.\n- **If the tool strips offsets,** row 3 becomes 04:10 and row 1 becomes 10:00. That gives the same several-hour gap.\n\nTaken as written, the three events are within about 10 minutes of each other (10:00Z, 10:05 if the naive row is UTC, and 10:10Z). They look hours apart only because of the mixed formats.\n\n**Fix**\n1. Normalize every timestamp to UTC at load time. In pandas, that's `pd.to_datetime(df.ts, utc=True)`.\n2. Decide what the naive rows mean. If the exporter writes UTC without the `Z`, treat them as UTC. If it writes local time, you need to know which zone.\n3. Better still, fix the exporter so it always emits ISO 8601 with an explicit offset.\n\nI only have 3 sample rows. If the real export is larger, or you can show me the chart script, I can check whether the jumps match this pattern or come from something else, like daylight-saving shifts or the chart's display timezone.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "bef